<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Document xmlns:p="property" xmlns:r="reflection" Id="RaTE04XdIRsb2s6149EGeK" LanguageVersion="2025.7.4" Version="0.128">
  <NugetDependency Id="DWcaH3grCjG4esnVdyXWNd" Location="VL.CoreLib" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="Os7VxTyfTN4d6YhsoLsWgY" Location="VL.Skia" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="Qnh6G0kq3f3Nj1ZfTQxGz4" Location="VL.Mapsui" Version="0.0.0" />
  <!--
    NetTopologySuite is declared HERE, by this document, and that is not the same as VL.Mapsui
    declaring it. A patch that uses NTS nodes itself needs its own dependency on the library, or
    the nodes below are "Not found". Measured in Tutorial 07, kept true here.
  -->
  <NugetDependency Id="HTLIbkV58Tnc4JBUrop3UY" Location="NetTopologySuite" Version="2.6.0" />
  <NugetDependency Id="CZjLFJs1YY8dEIks7FwB8a" Location="VL.NetTopologySuite" Version="0.0.0" />
  <Patch Id="LBwXs9zJoRUTxxur1nXgDa">
    <Canvas Id="Q7pXnwKPexnUm2z8E8Ks5K" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="AQDpXJU59pzl6KWNi7Ngof">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="OIcWIJAcQAyzJQu7iaFUeT">
        <Canvas Id="KvHjx135pCMx1bdQ6Racvv" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="JQx8mb4PsLwnlNMbRji3cq" Bounds="-780,-120,640,1660" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="08 - The map is just giving you coordinates.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Open the renderer and move the mouse over the map. Under LONGITUDE and LATITUDE, two numbers&#xD;&#xA;follow your cursor: the map is answering a question, sixty times a second - 'which place on the&#xD;&#xA;earth is under this pixel?' That answer is a node called ScreenToWorld, and everything else in&#xD;&#xA;this chapter is just things YOU do with it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;FIRST, CHAPTER 01 AGAIN, UNCHANGED. Coordinate, Point, Write WKT - your cursor is data again,&#xD;&#xA;and this time the data means somewhere. Copy the WKT box: that string is a real place, and&#xD;&#xA;every GIS tool on earth can read it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THEN CHAPTER 05, ON THE EARTH. Buffer grows your cursor's point into a circle - and Feature to&#xD;&#xA;FeatureLayer hands it back to the map, which draws it AS A PLACE. No pixels anywhere: your&#xD;&#xA;geometry lives in longitude and latitude, and projecting it onto the screen is the map's job,&#xD;&#xA;not yours. The same chain as chapter 07, now running live off your hand.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE TILES ARE OFF WHEN YOU ARRIVE (Enabled starts false, as always: opening a patch is running&#xD;&#xA;it, and you have not agreed to network traffic yet). Notice what already works: the numbers,&#xD;&#xA;the WKT, the teal circle. The map's geometry machinery never needed the pictures - switch&#xD;&#xA;Enabled on and the earth fades in underneath your circle.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;PLAY&#xD;&#xA;  Zoom in, zoom out, and watch the circle. On the screen it grows and shrinks. On the earth it&#xD;&#xA;  never changes size - it is a real patch of ground, about half a kilometre across, and the&#xD;&#xA;  Radius box says so in degrees. A drawing sticks to the window; a place sticks to the world.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Park the mouse and drag the map slowly: the numbers change even though the cursor never&#xD;&#xA;  moved. Which one moved - you, or the world?&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Make a circle permanent: put a Sample and Hold between Point and Buffer and gate it on&#xD;&#xA;  MouseState's Right Pressed. Now right-click stamps the circle onto that ground, and dragging&#xD;&#xA;  the map afterwards proves it stays there. HowTo Pick what you clicked recommends exactly&#xD;&#xA;  this pattern, for exactly this reason.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHAT HAPPENED?&#xD;&#xA;  The map stopped being a picture and became a question you can ask every frame. ScreenToWorld&#xD;&#xA;  is the map as a SENSOR; the Feature to FeatureLayer chain from chapter 07 is the map as a&#xD;&#xA;  PROJECTOR. Your geometry never touches a pixel - and that is not a limitation, it is the&#xD;&#xA;  design: every GIS keeps this division of labour, and so does this one.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  One thing to watch, carried from 06 and 07: LAYERS BUILT stays at 1. The circle is rebuilt&#xD;&#xA;  from your cursor every frame; the LAYER never is - contents are cheap, identity is not.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS&#xD;&#xA;  screen space   pixels from the window's top-left corner. Yours, and gone next frame.&#xD;&#xA;  world space    longitude and latitude on the earth (WGS84 - the GPS numbers). Everyone's.&#xD;&#xA;  projection     the arithmetic between the two. Web maps use Web Mercator (EPSG:3857);&#xD;&#xA;                 ScreenToWorld does that crossing for you and hands you WGS84 (EPSG:4326),&#xD;&#xA;                 the coordinates every dataset in chapter 09 speaks.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  And the honest caveat, carried from Act I: DEGREES ARE NOT METRES. The Radius box is in&#xD;&#xA;  degrees, so your 'circle' is really a slightly tall ellipse on the ground - a degree of&#xD;&#xA;  longitude shrinks as you leave the equator, and nothing in this family reprojects yet.&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 10 shows that lesson whole - three projections, one knob, Greenland changing size -&#xD;&#xA;  and Explanation 'The map is not to scale' measures this very circle's stretch, live, on a&#xD;&#xA;  real map.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;CREATIVE CONNECTION&#xD;&#xA;  Cursor to location, location to geometry, geometry to map: that loop is inside every&#xD;&#xA;  measuring tool, drawing tool and map editor ever shipped. Unfolding, the map library written&#xD;&#xA;  for designers, teaches this conversion before it teaches data files - interaction is what a&#xD;&#xA;  live map is FOR.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT - three directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 09                    real data: many places at once, from a file or the network&#xD;&#xA;  HowTo Pick what you clicked    the other question: not WHERE is the cursor, but WHAT is under it&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 01                    the same three nodes, before the earth arrived">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
